<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Elgar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1857">1857</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Edward William Elgar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Edward Elgar was an English composer, born at Lower Broadheath near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a>, many of whose works entered the British and international concert repertoire. Self-taught, a Roman Catholic in Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and sensitive about his humble origins, he struggled for success until his forties, when the Enigma Variations (1899) became popular in Britain and abroad, followed by the choral work The Dream of Gerontius (1900). He also wrote the Pomp and Circumstance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marches</a>, two symphonies and concertos for violin and cello, and was appointed Master of the King's Musick in 1924. Described as the first composer to take the gramophone seriously, he recorded most of his major orchestral works.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-elgar.jpg" alt="Edward Elgar" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Elgar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Elgar's father, William Henry Elgar, was raised in Dover and apprenticed to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> music publisher. In 1841 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a>, where he worked as a piano tuner and kept a shop selling sheet music and instruments. Edward was the fourth of seven children. His mother, Ann Elgar, had converted to Roman Catholicism shortly before his birth, and he was baptised and brought up in that faith, to his father's disapproval. William Elgar was a violinist of professional standard and organist of St George's Roman Catholic Church, Worcester, from 1846 to 1885.</p><p>At 29 Elgar took a pupil, Caroline Alice Roberts, eight years his senior. They married on 8 May 1889 at Brompton Oratory. Her family was horrified, and she was disinherited. She acted as his business manager and social secretary, and their daughter Carice Irene was born on 14 August 1890.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the 1890s Elgar built a reputation with works for the choral festivals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">English Midlands</a>, among them The Black Knight (1892), King Olaf (1896) and Caractacus (1898), and gained a long-standing publisher in Novello and Co. For the Birmingham Triennial Music Festival of 1900 he set Cardinal John Henry Newman's poem The Dream of Gerontius. Hans Richter conducted the premiere, in which a poorly prepared chorus sang badly. Performances in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> in 1901 and 1902 drew an enthusiastic German press, and Richard Strauss proposed a toast to him in his presence.</p><p>Some of the Anglican establishment disliked the Catholic text. Charles Villiers Stanford complained that it stank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>, and the dean of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a> banned it from his cathedral in 1901. Elgar was knighted in 1904, appointed to the Order of Merit in June 1911 and created a baronet in 1931. Alice Elgar died of lung cancer on 7 April 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Elgar was contemptuous of folk music. The continental composers who most influenced him were Handel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonin-dvorak">Dvořák</a> and, to some degree, Brahms, and his orchestration owes much to French composers, above all Delibes. His best-known works were written between 1899 and 1920, most of them orchestral, among them Cockaigne (1900–1901) and the Introduction and Allegro for Strings (1905). He completed no large-scale works after the Cello Concerto.</p><p>Between 1914 and 1925 he conducted acoustic recordings of his music, and after the moving-coil microphone arrived in 1923 he re-recorded most of his major orchestral works. His reputation fell after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and revived significantly from the 1960s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Elgar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Edward Elgar</b> re-scored it for very large orchestra for the Leeds Festival in 1922, and his version is the one usually heard at the Last Night of the Proms.</blockquote><a href="/songs/and-did-those-feet-in-ancient-time">Jerusalem (And did those feet in ancient time)</a></li><li><blockquote>The tune is the broad &quot;trio&quot; melody of <b>Edward Elgar</b>'s Pomp and Circumstance March No.</blockquote><a href="/songs/land-of-hope-and-glory">Land of Hope and Glory</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lower Broadheath, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Worcester, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.21,-2.27&amp;year=1907" aria-label="Lower Broadheath on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4499 361 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Edward Elgar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4949 631h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4949 631h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.21,-2.27&amp;year=1907">What was here in 1907? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
